Purpose  

 

The purpose of this paper is to define the Office of Performance and Quality’s (OPQ) position on the implementation of Artificial Intelligence (AI).  Existing Department of Homeland Security (DHS) and US Citizenship and Immigration Service (USCIS) documentation will be discussed and evaluated to identify existing constraints and gaps.  And finally, the document will seek to standardize governance and documentation in order to expand AI adoption within OPQ. 

 

Objectives 

 

The objectives of this paper are to: 

Provide an overview of AI tools currently available to the workforce 

Explore possible USCIS AI Use Cases 

Outline OPQ guidance and standards related to the use of AI 

Identify gaps in existing policy/standards  

Summarize current guidance provided through Presidential Executive Order, and various directives 

 

 

 

Project Scope 

 

The project scope for the Office of Performance and Quality’s (OPQ) AI Position paper is to evaluate existing AI policy, and illustrate potential benefits to the agency.  At the time of writing, the primary documents available are DHS Directives and Presidential Executive Orders.  These documents provide high level guidance and requirements for implementing AI across the agency.  The USCIS Chief Technology Office (CTO) is in the process of drafting documents and processes to align with those requirements.  At the time of writing there are few USCIS internal documents, this paper provides guidance until those documents are published.  Additionally, at the time of this writing DHS Chat is the only AI tool widely available to USCIS employees.  Additional tools are either highly specific to a use case (ie. ELIS photo validation) or are currently being evaluated for use.   

All capabilities and restrictions described within this paper were current at the time of it’s writing, May 7, 2026, and may have changed in the interim.   

 

Definitions 

Executive Order EO 13960 defines AI as: 

Any artificial system that performs tasks under varying and unpredictable circumstances without significant human oversight, or that can learn from experience and improve performance when exposed to data sets.  

An artificial system developed in computer software, physical hardware, or other context that solves tasks requiring human-like perception, cognition, planning, learning, communication, or physical action.  

An artificial system designed to think or act like a human, including cognitive architectures and neural networks.  

A set of techniques, including machine learning that is designed to approximate a cognitive task.  

An artificial system is designed to act rationally, including an intelligent software agent or embodied robot that achieves goals using perception, planning, reasoning, learning, communicating, decision-making, and acting.  

