OMB has outlined Generative AI Guidance (Generative AI Acceptable Use Guidelines Template, Version 1, December 2025). 

General Principles 

1. Accountability: End users of AI are responsible for the quality, accuracy, and reliability of work products, regardless of if they were developed with GenAI. All outputs must be manually reviewed for accuracy, consistency, and intellectual property (IP) compliance. 

2. Transparency: Users must clearly disclose the use of GenAI in developing work products, including by labeling outputs generated or assisted by GenAI, such as text, audio, imagery, or other data, [insert agency-specific language] with appropriate markings according to the Labeling Guide (see example in Appendix i). Users should use existing redress courses to escalate and review important decisions. See Human In The Loop. 

3. Human In The Loop: Users must maintain human validation protocols to ensure all decisions and content made or influenced by GenAI are evaluated by humans. 

4. Explainability: Users must ensure operations and outcomes of AI use cases are sufficiently explainable, as appropriate. Subject matter experts should understand, where possible, the reasoning behind the decisions or predictions made, depending on the subject matter and risk posture. Explainability becomes a higher priority as the risk level increases, and High-Impact Use Cases may need a higher level of explainability. 

5. Public Trust: Users are responsible for avoiding using GenAI to generate inappropriate, misleading, offensive, or illegal material 

 

Data Protection and Privacy 

1. Permitted Data for Public Tools: users may input strictly unclassified information into public GenAI tools. This includes information that is already in public-facing websites and information that is not Sensitive But Unclassified (SBU) [add agency-specific data types]. 

2. Permitted Data for Internal Tools: When using Department GenAI tools, users may only input data up to the allowable sensitivity level for the specific Gen AI tool (i.e., do not put classified information into systems rated for unclassified or SBU information only). Refrain from leveraging internal tools for personal use. 

3. Data and Records Retention: users may create federal records when using AI. Inputs and outputs from GenAI tools that constitute federal records must be stored and reviewed for compliance and enhancement purposes. End users must ensure proper data classification and marking for dissemination. Records created using GenAI will be retained according to Record Retention Policy Guidance found here [add link to your agency policy]. [If needed, see example in Appendix II, ii]. 

4. Privacy-Enhancing Measures: For specific guidance on protecting certain data types, such as PII, the AI Use Case Owner should consult with the Privacy Officer to determine if any of the following applies: Privacy Act restrictions or constraints; Fair Information Practice Principles (FIPPs) in OMB Circular A-130, Appendix II;) Privacy Impact Assessment (PIA) per Section 208 of the E-Government Act; Committee on National Security Systems Instruction (CNSSI) No. 1253, Privacy Overlays; NIST SP 800-66, Rev. 2; or privacy-enhancing cryptograph techniques. 

 

Prohibited Generative AI Use Cases 

 

OMB has created a document outlining prohibited AI Use Cases (Generative AI Acceptable Use Guidelines Template, Version 1, December 2025). 

Unacceptable use cases include: 

1. Inherently Governmental Functions: GenAI must not be the sole performer of inherently governmental functions, such as final hiring decisions, grant scoring and reviewing, enforcement actions, and benefits administration, among others. GenAI should not make decisions for High Impact Use Cases. 

2. Misleading Information: Users may not use GenAI to create false or misleading content or alter original artifacts deceptively. 

3. Harmful Bias and Discrimination: Users may not use GenAI to produce ideologically and/or partisan biased information or outputs that are incomplete or contradictory. 

4. Recreating specific individuals: Follow agency policy. In general, users may not generate images of specific people without their explicit permission (e.g., "redraw this photo of my coworker in an animated style"). Users may not create images of minors. 

5. Copyrighted Materials: Follow agency policy. In general, users must always obtain material for GenAI inputs lawfully and may not use GenAI to obtain access to or unlawfully use copyrighted materials. 

 

Use of PII Data 

 

OMB has outlined special considerations when using PII Data (OMB Best Practices of Generative AI within Federal Agencies, Version 2: December 2025) 

 

If your project involves the use of PII, consult with your agency Privacy Officer to determine if that: 

1) triggers any Privacy Act restrictions or constraints; 

2) is consistent with the Fair Information Practice Principles (FIPPs) in OMB Circular A-130, Appendix II; 

3) requires creation or modification of a Privacy Impact Assessment (PIA) per E-Government Act, Section 208; 

4) requires application of one or more of the Committee on National Security Systems Instruction (CNSSI) No. 1253, Privacy Overlays; 

5) involves protected health information (PHI) and requires application of NIST SP 800-66, Rev. 2 to the project; and, 

6) necessitates use of privacy-enhancing cryptograph techniques in addition to differential privacy methods to improve the privacy posture for the project. 

 

Compliance and Monitoring 

 

OMB has outlined guidance on complying with policies and how AI solutions should be created and monitored post-production. (OMB Generative AI Acceptable Use Guidelines Template, Version 1, December 2025). 

 

1. Adherence to Policies: Users must follow all organizational policies related to cybersecurity, privacy, and data and IT governance in Department policy throughout the AI lifecycle. For instance, if using a commercially available tool for work purposes, use a government account, rather than a personal account. A full list of policies and guidelines may be found [enter the location where policies are found. Relevant policies are: scientific integrity, FOIA, etc.]. 

2. System Specific Policies: Users must follow all AI service, system, and tool-specific policies and guidelines. For questions, users should contact the tool owner or the AI Use Case owner. 

3. No Expectation of Privacy and Monitoring: Users have no expectation of privacy when using Department systems, which are continually monitored. 

4. Cybersecurity Incidents: Users must report any suspected data spillage, compromise, or inappropriate use of GenAI tools to the Cyber Incident Response Team via unclassified email at [add email address here] or classified email at [add email address here]. 

5. End User Feedback: Users should provide feedback on output quality and system functionality to the Use Case owners for system improvements. 

6. Training: Users are encouraged to leverage training regarding best practices or to reach out to the agency Chief AI Officer with specific questions about applied use. 

 

