Appendix D: USCIS Governance and Procedures 

 

The CTO is currently developing USCIS governance. Expected within this FY.   

 

The following USCIS governance entities are involved in the AI review process: 

USCIS Chief Artificial Intelligence Officer (CAIO) – Ericka Botts 

In accordance with Executive Order 14179 and OMB Memo M-25-21, USCIS has designated a Chief Artificial Intelligence Officer (CAIO), Erika Botts, to provide centralized leadership and governance over the agency’s use of AI.  

The CAIO serves as the senior advisor to agency leadership on AI strategy, policy, and decision-making, ensuring that AI initiatives align with the mission, legal requirements, and agency standards. In this capacity, the CAIO works in close coordination with the Office of Information Technology (OIT) to promote an integrated and enterprise-wide approach to AI adoption and oversight. 

The CAIO chairs the agency’s AI Governance Board (AIGB), making the CAIO responsible for coordinating with standing members, governance entities, and various other program offices to manage risks associated with AI systems, conduct and oversee risk assessments of AI application, and ensure adherence to risk management requirements. 

 

USCIS Artificial Intelligence Governance Board (AIGB) 

USCIS established the AIGB to oversee the responsible use of AI within the agency. Chaired by the CAIO, the AIGB consists of standing members from key offices and functions: 

Office of Chief Counsel (OCC) 

Office of the Chief Data Officer (OCDO) 

Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) 

Office of Information Technology (OIT) 

Office of Policy and Strategy (OP&S) 

Human Capital and Training (HCT) 

Labor and Employee Relations (LER) 

Privacy Office (PRIV) 

The AIGB collaborates with key governance entities to ensure compliance with federal and DHS mandates, foster responsible AI use, and establish risk management frameworks. The board’s authority is limited to governance and oversight of AI use. Technical assessments and approvals, including system architecture alignment, cybersecurity posture, infrastructure, and tool selection remain under the responsibility of the appropriate technical and security authorities within the agency. 

 

Technical Architecture Board (TAB) 

The TAB is the body responsible for governing Information Technology (IT) architecture activities and approving certain IT requests. The TAB fulfills its mission through two main roles: 

Decision-making body for new software asset requests; and 

Reviewing body for new business IT capability requests submitted through the OIT Information Technology Demand Management (ITDM) process. 

 

 

